      commonMistakes: {
        id: 'common-mistakes',
        title: 'Common Mistakes When Trying SGLang',
        content: 'Most friction with SGLang comes from treating it like a single-user desktop tool, or from expecting RadixAttention to help a workload that does not actually share prefixes.',
        subsections: [
          {
            title: 'Mistake 1: Trying to run it on a laptop with no dedicated GPU',
            content: 'SGLang is not built around CPU-only or Apple Silicon hardware the way llama.cpp or Ollama is. Attempting to run it on a CPU-only laptop or an Apple Silicon Mac typically leads to poor performance or setup friction that a purpose-built local tool would avoid entirely.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Mistake 2: Expecting RadixAttention to speed up traffic with no shared prefixes',
            content: 'RadixAttention\'s benefit comes specifically from reusing KV-cache state across requests that share a token-sequence prefix. If every request in your workload is fully independent — different system prompts, no few-shot examples, no conversation history — there is little or no shared prefix for RadixAttention to reuse, and the gain over a non-caching engine will be small.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Mistake 3: Asking for JSON in the prompt instead of using the structured-output parameters',
            content: 'Prompting a model to "respond in JSON" without using SGLang\'s schema-constrained decoding parameters still lets the model produce malformed or non-conformant output. The reliability benefit of engine-level structured output only applies when the JSON schema or regex constraint is actually passed through the API\'s structured-output fields.',
          },
        ],
      },
      faq: {
        id: 'faq',
        title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
        faqs: [
          { q: 'What is SGLang?', a: 'SGLang is a free, Apache 2.0-licensed serving framework for large language models and vision-language models, originated by researchers connected to UC Berkeley, Stanford, and the LMSYS organization behind Chatbot Arena. It is best known for RadixAttention, a technique for automatic KV-cache reuse across requests sharing a common prefix.' },
          { q: 'Is SGLang free?', a: 'Yes. SGLang is free, open-source software released under the Apache 2.0 license, with no subscription or account requirement to run it yourself.' },
          { q: 'What is RadixAttention?', a: 'RadixAttention is SGLang\'s technique for storing the attention KV cache from completed and in-flight requests in a radix tree, so that new requests sharing a token-sequence prefix — a system prompt, few-shot examples, or earlier conversation turns — can automatically reuse the matching cache instead of recomputing it.' },
          { q: 'Does SGLang guarantee valid JSON output?', a: 'When a request includes a JSON schema in SGLang\'s structured-output parameters, the engine masks out tokens that would violate the schema at each decoding step, which is designed to make the output conform to the schema by construction rather than by post-hoc validation. Simply asking for JSON in the prompt text without using these parameters does not get this guarantee.' },
          { q: 'Does SGLang need a GPU?', a: 'For any real workload, yes — SGLang\'s primary and best-supported target is NVIDIA GPUs. The project documents AMD, Intel, and other accelerator backends, but they are not the main deployment path, and there is no first-class Apple Silicon support.' },
          { q: 'What quantization formats does SGLang support?', a: 'SGLang supports several formats including FP8, FP4 on newer hardware, AWQ, GPTQ, and INT4, with many pre-quantized models in these formats published on Hugging Face.' },
          { q: 'Is SGLang better than vLLM?', a: 'Neither project\'s own benchmarks are a neutral verdict on this — both publish results favorable to themselves. SGLang emphasizes RadixAttention\'s prefix-sharing cache reuse and engine-level structured output as its headline features; vLLM emphasizes PagedAttention\'s memory efficiency. Which is a better fit depends on your workload\'s prefix-sharing pattern and whether structured output is a hard requirement — see the comparison table above.' },
          { q: 'Does SGLang have an OpenAI-compatible API?', a: 'Yes. Running `python -m sglang.launch_server` starts a server implementing the OpenAI API protocol, so many applications built for the OpenAI API can point at a self-hosted SGLang instance with only a base-URL and model-name change.' },
          { q: 'What is the SGLang frontend DSL used for?', a: 'It is a set of Python primitives — including `sgl.gen`, `sgl.select`, and `sgl.fork` — for writing multi-step LLM programs, such as branching into several parallel sub-generations and merging the results, as ordinary Python code rather than manually orchestrating separate API calls.' },
        ],
      },
